  "interpretation": [
    "The raw per-SD specification is unreliable on its own: k has skewness 7.28 and an SD (0.0165) larger than its median (0.00995), so a one-SD contrast is dominated by a few extreme values. The null must be read from the better-specified forms, not from M1 alone.",
    "Better-specified continuous forms all point the hypothesised way but stay above 0.05: log-rate HR 0.870 (p=0.207), rank-normalised HR 0.825 (p=0.096), ordinal quartile trend HR 0.846 per quartile (p=0.105).",
    "Only the median dichotomisation reaches significance (HR 0.595 [0.377,0.940], p=0.026; log-rank p=0.012; 16.6 vs 20.9 months). This is post-hoc, survives only a narrow contiguous cutpoint window (4 of 13 cutpoints scanned, 45th-60th percentile), and does not survive Bonferroni correction across the six specifications run (p_adj=0.156).",
    "Immortal-time bias is excluded as an explanation: a week-15 landmark analysis removes only 1 patient and leaves every estimate essentially unchanged.",
    "The cohort is a 15-week survivor population (KM median OS 19.8 months vs 14.9 months arm-wide), because a rate estimate requires >=3 PSA values in the first 15 weeks. This limits generalisability, though it biases all comparison groups equally.",
    "This is an underpowered null, not a demonstration of absence: 78 events give 80% power only down to HR 0.53 (binary) or 0.73 (per SD). A true modest effect of HR ~0.80-0.85 would very likely have been missed.",
    "The earlier PSA-response (PSARS) anchor must be downgraded: PSARS is defined as a >=50% PSA decline and k is the PSA decline rate over the same window, so that anchor is within-modality and cannot establish clinical validity on its own."
  ],
